# Rust build output
/target/
/target-verify/
**/*.rs.bk

# The client's asset archive when a developer keeps a local copy in this repository (data/Gw.dat or
# ./Gw.dat). It is the game's file, not this project's; the world reads it for path graphs, and a
# build without one still serves maps, it just cannot answer pathfinding.
/data/
/Gw.dat

# Local run artifacts: logs, captures, recordings, configs with secrets
*.log
/logs/
/captures/
*.jsonl
/config.local.*
# ...except the reference capture the offline checks read: it is a committed fixture, not a run log.
!tools/fixtures/*.log

# Local secrets and configuration. The real .env is never committed; the template is.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pgpass

# Editors and OS
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Probe and comparison artifacts: captures of what a server sent, written by the diagnostics in
# tools/ and crates/asura-auth/examples/probe.rs. Reproducible in a minute, so they do not belong in
# the history -- the reference *session* they were distilled into is docs/06-reference-session.md.
.reply-*.bin
.gwps-reply.bin
.our-reply.bin
.probe-*.txt
.reference-session.txt
.gwps-session.log
*.err

# Python bytecode from the tools/ scripts
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The oracles: host tools compiled out of the reference's own sources (tools/path-oracle.c,
# tools/pack-oracle.c) by tools/build-oracle.ps1. What they print is committed as a fixture instead; a
# binary is rebuilt when someone wants to check the fixture against the reference again.
/tools/path-oracle.exe
/tools/path-oracle.obj
/tools/path-oracle.pdb
/tools/pack-oracle.exe
/tools/pack-oracle.obj
/tools/pack-oracle.pdb
